WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:05.550
Tests can also be used
to make comparisons,

2
00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:08.530
and are often used to compare
fairly simple things:

3
00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:13.750
for example, the difference in means
between two independent groups.

4
00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:18.430
Here, again, using our short
piece of R code, we have generated

5
00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:22.250
heights for a population.

6
00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:25.330
We have one group of six individuals
whose heights we recorded,

7
00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:28.650
and another group, B, of six individuals
whose heights we also recorded.

8
00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.770
We want to know whether
the mean height in group

9
00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:35.910
A differs significantly
from the mean in group B.

10
00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:38.710
Again, we use a

11
00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:44.930
t-test to compare the means
of these two samples.

12
00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:52.260
If the probability
associated with the result

13
00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:54.860
of this test is below 0.05,

14
00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:59.580
we reject the hypothesis that the two
groups have the same mean.

15
00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.040
Otherwise, we accept
that they have the same mean.

16
00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:06.060
If our sample did not follow
a normal distribution,

17
00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:11.140
we would prefer a Wilcoxon test
for non-parametric data to the t-test.

18
00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:13.680
We can use this boxplot to visualise

19
00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:16.840
the difference in heights
between our two groups.

20
00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:22.980
Suppose that, in this particular
case, we had concluded

21
00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:26.480
that we should reject
equality of the two means.

22
00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:29.330
We can carry out the same kind of test

23
00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:34.270
not just to compare the means
of two samples or groups,

24
00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:38.090
but also within the same
sample with, say, three groups.

25
00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:41.250
Here we generate an example

26
00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:46.760
simulating agricultural experiments,

27
00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:49.585
with three types of fertiliser: A, B, C,

28
00:01:49.585 --> 00:01:53.940
D. After a certain time,
we calculate the biomass,

29
00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:57.400
the growth of plants under
these three types of fertiliser.

30
00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:02.140
With fertiliser
A, the biomass values in five

31
00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:07.060
separate plots range from 6 to 10.

32
00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:12.220
With fertiliser B,
the values range from 10 to 14.

33
00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:15.440
With fertiliser C,
they range from 16 to 20.

34
00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:18.820
This is entirely hypothetical:
these data were

35
00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:24.240
generated to demonstrate
how to use the method.

36
00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:28.180
To compare more than two means,

37
00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:31.960
we cannot use the t-test.
We use analysis of variance

38
00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:37.220
to compare the means of more
than two groups at once.

39
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:42.600
The null hypothesis is that all
the means are equal.

40
00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:46.360
The mean of A equals the mean of B,
which equals the mean of C.

41
00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:51.700
If one of these means
differs, we reject H0.

42
00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:57.660
The non-parametric counterpart
is the Kruskal–Wallis test,

43
00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:02.940
which works with the ordering
of the data rather than the raw values.

44
00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:09.400
Let us spend a moment on the ANOVA example

45
00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:11.960
to see how a test is constructed.

46
00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:16.380
ANOVA is based on Fisher's test,

47
00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:21.420
which examines the difference
between the variation

48
00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:26.860
between groups
and the variation within groups.

49
00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:31.380
We can express this using
the formula shown here:

50
00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:37.400
the total sum of squares is the sum of

51
00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:41.740
each observation's deviation
from the overall mean,

52
00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:44.860
shown by this dashed line,
with each difference squared

53
00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:50.160
so that positive and negative
deviations do not cancel out.

54
00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:54.900
This total variation
can be split into variation

55
00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:58.980
within groups: each observation
relative to its group mean,

56
00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:03.840
and between groups: each group
mean relative to the overall mean.

57
00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:05.820
So the total sum of squares

58
00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:10.200
can be split into between-group
and within-group sums of squares.

59
00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:13.540
In this example, the total sum

60
00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:18.200
of squares is 283, split into 253 and 30.

61
00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:22.600
For the F-test, we must account
for the degrees of freedom.

62
00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:25.840
We have three groups,
so we take n minus 1,

63
00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:32.420
and divide the between-group
sum of squares by 2.

64
00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:36.320
For the within-group sum of squares,
we take the number of observations

65
00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:50.480
minus 3, giving us 30 divided by 12.

66
00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:56.310
F is therefore 50.67,
which tells us nothing on its own.

67
00:04:56.630 --> 00:05:01.230
But if we compare it with the known
F distribution, with 2

68
00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:07.210
and 12 degrees of freedom,
we can see that, if we assume

69
00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:13.570
under H0 that all the group
means are equal, we reject

70
00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:20.170
that hypothesis and accept that at least
one group has a different mean.
